A||||3||Carrolles||Lessons Learned||||1||
Z||000000||Carrolles||01-18-2000||01:12 PM||||I just thought I'd pass on a few lessons learned so maybe some of you don't have to learn the hard way.<P>Transmission Axel Seals:<BR>When I replaced my clutch and installed a new engine I decided to change the axel seals on my 85SE 4spd Muncie.  The seals cost about $4.50 each and are fairly easy to replace especially when the trans is out of the car.  I used a seal puller that looks like half of a claw hammer claw.  It worked great at reaching in and grabbing the seal.<BR>When I got the cradle/engine/trans back in the car and filled the trans with oil I had a slight drip from the left seal.  It seamed to be coming from between the seal and the trans housing rather than between the seal and the axel.  I noticed that I did not have the seal completely bottomed out so I pulled it out and installed a new one and made sure to get it pounded in all the way.<BR>Guess what?  Same drip.<BR>When I pulled it again I found that I had gotten a bit too aggressive with the seal puller and had scratched the inside bore of the transmission case opening where the seal installs.  When I installed the new seal I put some black RTV on it being careful not to let any get into the case.  So far so good.<P>Muncie 4spd speedometer gear:<BR>On the Muncie 4spd you have to pull the vehicle speed sensor (VSS) out of the transmission case to check the oil level in the transmission.  The speedometer gear is only slightly smaller in diameter than the hole that it is installed through.  When pulling the sensor out the gear often hangs up on the opening until I finally get it lined up perfectly.  I never thought much about this until this last time I pulled it out.  It hung on the opening.  As I carefully pulled it out of the case the gear came off of the shaft and fell on the ground.<BR>Looking at the gear I noticed that the only thing holding it onto the VSS shaft is a small tab which is part of the plastic gear.  Had I not been lucky this time the gear would have fallen back into the gear case.  Needless to say this would be a bad thing.  I've heard of people losing their speedo gears in the case and having to crack the trans case open to fish it out.<P>Brake hoses:<BR>While I was replacing the trans axel seal last weekend I noticed a worn spot on the brake hose of the left rear brake.  I had forgotten to bolt the tab on the brake hose to the strut.  Consequently the brake hose rubbed on the strut and wore a spot into the jacket.<P>Starter Motor:<BR>I had just bolted my new engine (v-6) onto the transmission (Muncie 4spd) and was pretty excited about the thought of getting the car back on the road after 2-1/2 months of working on Saturdays when I would be at home for the weekend.  I got everything bolted up and it was looking great.<BR>When I tried to install the lower dust shield on the end of the bell housing I realized that the starter was in the way and had to be removed.  Rats!  I thought I was being smart by installing the starter while the engine was still on the stand.  Oops.<P>Cooling pipe:<BR>I also thought it was a good idea to install the new A/C compressor while the engine was on the stand.  Guess what?  I had to remove it to get the cooling crossover pipe hooked up once the engine was bolted to the cradle.  Oh well.<P>I've got to get back to work now.  As I think of new oh ohs I'll post them here.||||reg||1
Z||000001||mshill||01-18-2000||02:55 PM||||Carrolles,  great information to know since I have my 86SE V6 block on a stand with only the cam, crank and pistons remaining to be removed.  Over the next three weeks I will be cleaning parts and putting them back together after the block and heads get back from the machine shop.||||reg||4
Z||000002||penetrator||01-18-2000||03:52 PM||||Ive lost my speedogear also in the gearbox. <BR>But i had no place or afford the cost of cracking up my gearcase.<P>So, what i was doing was to drive with the gear in the tranny, and listen to the crispy sound when it was demolished. Its made of plastic. But i wasn't that lucky tho. My right axel seal started to leak, but after a mounth it seamd to seal itself.<P>Thanks<BR>Magnus||||reg||1
Z||000003||Carrolles||01-18-2000||04:39 PM||||Priming Oil Pump:<BR>When I finally got all the major parts installed on the block I decided to fill it with oil and prime the oil pump.  I duct taped a socket (I believe it was 10mm) to a 1/4" drive extension and chucked it in my cordless drill.  I stabbed it onto the oip pump drive shaft and started the slowly to get the pump primed.  It didn't take long before I had oil gushing out of the distributor mounting hole around the drill shaft.  I forgot to place some washers over the hole to close it up.  I didn't lose much oil but then again a little oil goes a long way on a newly painted engine.<BR>I stopped and stacked several washers together over the hole to get a relatively tight fit between the OD of the hole and the drill shaft.  I had my Wife turn the shaft with the drill whild I turned the crankshaft by hand through about 5 revolutions.<P>Exhaust Manifold Gaskets:<BR>The exhaust manifold gasket that came in the set has an offset strip connecting the three points that seal the exhaust ports.  Not knowing any better I installed the gasket with the connecting strip on the top.  When I installed the spark plugs this was in the way.  I cut them out to make room for the plug wires.<P>V-6 Intake Plenum:<BR>The 4 part article about stroking the 2.8l v-6 that ren in the FOCOA magazine mentioned that the arrow cast into the surface of the intake plenum should face the distributor side of the engine (or was it the other end?   I don't remember)  Anyway it was wrong.  When bolted in that manner the boltholes for some of the accessories edn up on the wrong side of the engine.  Luckily all I had to do was to take it off (remove something like 10 bolts that I had painstakingly torqued) and turn it around.<P>Water Pump:<BR>Here is one that I didn't have to learn the hard way.  Before I removed the water pump I traced the outline of the new pump onto a piece of cardboard and cut holes at each of the mounting bolt locations.  As I removed the bolts I stuck them into the corresponding holes in the cardboard so as to remember where the belong on the real pump.  This sure paid off because several of the bolts look similar but are of slightly different lengths and some are studs instead of bolts.<P>Fuel Injectors:<BR>While I had the engine apart I cleaned everything and repainted everything back to original colors.  I cleaned the fuel injectors (only externally) and installed all new o-rings.  Pontiac wanted something like $30 for the o-ring set.  I bought them at PepBoys for 50 cents a piece (total $6.00).<P>A/C compressor:<BR>Since my A/C compressor had long ago locked up I bought a new AC Delco one through a local parts house near home.  I also got a R-134 conversion kit with an 8 oz. bottle of the oil, fittings, and an assortment of seals.  Since I was going to flush the entire system out and replace the drier, expansion valve, and every o-ring seal in the system I poured the entire 8 oz. of oil into the new compressor and put the plastic plugs back in to keep it from leaking out.<BR>I installed the compressor onto the engine while it was on the engine stand along with a new belt for it and the alternator.  I was just about to bump the motor over with a battery when my neighbor (who happens to be a Mazda mechanic) said Whoa!  I asked why and he told me that with the A/C belt installed it would blow all the oil right out of the compressor.  Good point.  Saved again from my own stupidity.<P>Leaving a Trail of Breadcrumbs:<BR>I had never pulled an engine all by myself before.  I've helped many others do it on their cars but I had never tackled the job alone.<BR>Before and all during the engine removal, rebuilding, and reinstalling I video taped the progress in detail.  i did this mainly to remember where everything came from and in what order it was removed.<BR>It sure came in handy when it came time to install the vacuum harness and the wiring harness.  As I filmed I talked to the camera explaining what I was doing.<BR>If I had to do it again I'd film even more details. ||||reg||1
